<h2>The question</h2>
<p>
  PR #1607 (landed as card <a href="/backlog/3351-validate-a-juror-s-cited-file-against-the-net-diff-and-cover/">#3351</a>)
  added 25 adversarial fixtures to the review pipeline. <strong>Six found real defects.</strong> Should Web
  Everything carry a durable, cite-able acceptance criterion requiring adversarial fixtures for machinery that
  consumes model output — and if so, what is the <strong>bound class</strong>, how is <strong>membership</strong>
  established, and what <strong>qualifies</strong> a fixture set? This topic grounds that decision.
</p>

<h2>The load-bearing observation — none of the six was a parse failure</h2>
<p>
  Every one of the six inputs was <strong>structurally valid JSON</strong>. Conventional malformed-input
  testing would have caught none of them. The defects lived in output that was
  <strong>semantically wrong but structurally valid</strong> — which is exactly what a real model produces when
  it is confidently wrong. Any criterion that reduces to "test malformed input" has missed the class entirely.
</p>

