    <tr>
      <td><strong>Structured-output tooling</strong> — Instructor, Outlines, Pydantic AI, OpenAI structured outputs, GBNF grammars</td>
      <td>Treats the model's response as a thing to validate before use</td>
      <td>Converged on <em>constrained decoding</em>: schema-invalid output becomes impossible <em>by construction</em>. That closes the entire parse-failure family and <strong>none</strong> of the six. The practitioner literature already names the residue — "valid structure, meaningless data" — and prescribes semantic validators plus <strong>verbatim evidence spans</strong>, because a quote is hard to hallucinate.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>OWASP LLM05:2025 Improper Output Handling</strong> (LLM02 in the 2023/2024 revision)</td>
      <td>The correct posture, stated plainly: <em>treat LLM output as untrusted data</em>; the model is an interpreter of untrusted input</td>
      <td>The wrong <em>threat model</em>. Its mitigations are injection sinks — escape SQL/HTML, strip command patterns, constrain formats — because the assumed harm is <strong>code execution</strong>. Here the harm is that the machinery <strong>reaches a wrong verdict and acts on it</strong>. No sink is involved in any of the six.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>LLM eval / golden-set practice</strong> — a version-controlled set of 50–200 stratified cases with an adversarial bucket and a shipped-failure replay bucket, gated in CI</td>
