<h2>The coercion is Postel's law at a trust boundary</h2>
<p>
  The defect at the heart of #3 and #4 is <code>Array.isArray(rawList) ? rawList : []</code> — "be liberal in
  what you accept," applied at the exact seam where liberality is harmful. The standing critique of the
  robustness principle is that <strong>liberal acceptance removes the feedback that would correct bad
  behaviour</strong>: a receiver that silently accepts bad input means the sender never learns. With a model as
  the sender there is no feedback loop at all within the run, so the blocker is not merely delayed — it is
  destroyed. "I would not merge this" sat unread in the summary while the pipeline recorded an accept.
</p>

<h2>The qualifying test — mutation, not taxonomy</h2>
<p>
  A named minimum set of fixture kinds (hallucinated reference · silent coercion · summary-vs-detail
  contradiction · volume · cross-seat disagreement) is <em>checkable</em> but ages with the model failure
  landscape, and admits a fixture that carries the right tag while asserting nothing. "Adversarial, author's
  judgement" is durable but unfalsifiable, and an unfalsifiable criterion is not a criterion.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Discrimination</strong> is the third option and it is both: a fixture counts <em>iff</em> there is a
  named line of guard code whose removal makes it fail. That definition cannot go stale (it references no
  taxonomy) and cannot be gamed (a fixture that discriminates nothing does not count). It is mutation testing's
  premise — <em>put the bug back and see whether anything goes red</em> — used as an admission test rather than
  as a coverage score.
</p>
<p>
  Web Everything has already reached this conclusion twice, independently: the tier-1 determinism qualifier
  ("if the criterion would still pass against a double that returns <code>''</code> for everything, it is not
  proving the mechanism") and a <strong>shipped</strong> <code>mutation-check</code> operation whose
  three-valued <code>killed</code> / <code>survived</code> / <code>unrun</code> outcome already handles the
  obvious gaming case — a find-pattern that matched nothing must never be reported as <code>killed</code>,
  because that "would certify a guard as sound on the strength of a test run that examined the unmodified
  code."
</p>

<h2>Placement — this discharges a documented hole, it does not open a new one</h2>
<p>
  The existing operation-IO fidelity gate enforces the tier-1 qualifier across the operations tree with a
  shrinking allowlist. Its debt register permanently exempts two modules with the reason stated in the source:
  <em>"declares a <code>judge</code> step: the real mechanism needs a model, not a repo."</em> Those two are
  exactly the modules a model-output rule would govern. So the proposed rule is the <strong>complement</strong>
  of the existing gate — the half that a real repo cannot prove and a fake model can — not a rival to it.
</p>

