<p>
  The fake-model harness's own header already names the gap and leaves the hook: <em>"It does not run a
  model… The quality of what an agent produces is a different test and a different budget — this is the
  harness that makes that test a swap of one binding rather than a new build."</em>
</p>

<h2>Recommendation</h2>
<p>
  <strong>Codify it as an amendment to an existing anchor, not a new one.</strong> The gap a new anchor would
  have claimed is already half-filled: the convergence-validation anchor's second clause requires
  <em>"a test that fails on pre-change behavior for logic fixes"</em>, scoped to <em>"any
  AI-review/convergence surface in the constellation"</em>. What that clause cannot reach is the
  <strong>trigger</strong> — it fires on a fix, and none of the six defects had one. So the amendment binds
  the same requirement to the consumer's <strong>existence</strong>.
</p>
<p>
  The <strong>class</strong> is defined by the trust boundary, never by failure shape — a scope that asks "is
  this failure silent?" is undecidable from a diff and will be argued away at review time. But the class is
  not reliably decidable from a single file either, so the <strong>enforced set is declared</strong>, widened
  by <strong>import-graph reachability from the single seam where a model response is parsed</strong>, and
  ratcheted with reasons on the line. A source scan for the semantic property was run against the tree and
  got it wrong in both directions — missing both motivating files while flagging a documented pure leaf —
  which is the same result this repo's existing fidelity gate had already recorded against itself.
</p>
<p>
  A fixture <strong>qualifies by discrimination</strong>, which is the convergence clause's own before/after
  bar restated in the form that works when no change is in flight. The five-kind failure taxonomy is
  therefore <em>not</em> part of the rule: a fixture carrying the right tag while asserting nothing would
  pass a taxonomy check and prove nothing. It stays here, as a prompt for an author.
</p>
